// F11 — the three ways one module reaches another, in one file.
//
//   bsc examples/Shop/Reports/Totals.bs Restate 3
//
// Nothing here names a path, a build order or an artefact. Ticket 41 §3 put the
// dependency graph in the compiler: `using` is resolved to source, that source is
// checked FIRST, and its signatures are kept in the environment this module is
// checked against. Correct by construction — there is no file that could go stale.
module Shop.Reports

// Module tier (41 §2): the names come in UNQUALIFIED. This is TypeScript's
// named-import semantics exactly, and C#'s middle tier does not exist here
// because B# has no namespace/type split to hang it on.
using Shop.Collections.List

// Namespace tier (41 §5): `Shop.Collections` holds no functions of its own, so it
// is a NAMESPACE — erased entirely, no atom, nothing emitted. It is a way of
// naming, not a thing that exists at run time.
using Shop.Collections

public int Restate(int n)
// `Sum` unqualified, through the module-tier import. It resolves at CHECK time
// and emits a remote call to 'Shop.Collections.List' — the resolution never
// happens at run time.
Restate(n) -> Sum([n, n, n], 0)

public int Counted(int n)
// The same callee named through the namespace tier instead. `List` here is short
// for `Shop.Collections.List`, resolved through the module table.
Counted(n) -> List.Length([n, n])

public int Fully(int n)
// And fully qualified, which is legal regardless of what is in scope — which is
// exactly why 41 §2 can require every DIAGNOSTIC to print this form and never
// have to know the call site's scope.
Fully(n) -> Shop.Collections.List.Sum([n], 0)
